+The user explicitly approved expanding the draft to cover:
+
+- a provider-browser broker for temporary accounts;
+- a short-lived, purpose-scoped handoff credential; and
+- the compatibility boundary between that broker and native mobile OAuth.
+
+The approval is limited to design and implementation planning. It does not
+authorize production-code edits, deployment, provider configuration, data
+migration execution, or replacement of native OAuth. Permanent accounts keep
+the existing `MobileOAuth` native callback contract and their existing stored
+grants throughout this work.
+
+This is consequential work because it adds a server return boundary, a new
+credential type, temporary identity and expiry state, and mixed-version
+compatibility. The plan therefore treats the broker as a separate owner and
+keeps the native adapter as a compatibility boundary rather than changing the
+permanent-account flow.
+
+## Authoritative ownership
+
+| Concept | Owner | Writers | Readers | Representations | Recovery/rebuild |
+| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
+| Account identity and temporary expiry | `accounts.py` account owner | account lifecycle service | broker, adapter, expiry worker | `Account`, durable account row | expiry state is authoritative; no callback recreates an expired account |
+| Provider-browser operation | provider-browser broker | broker begin/return handlers | temporary-account linking client, operator diagnostics | durable operation record | replay by operation id with compare-and-set terminal state |
+| Handoff credential | broker credential owner | issuance/revocation path | one broker return exchange | opaque signed or stored reference with typed fields | expiry/revocation state wins; it is never rebuilt as a product session |
+| Permanent native OAuth | `mobile_oauth.py` | current native begin/complete path | released mobile clients and provider adapters | existing callback and registry grant records | existing registry remains authoritative |
+| Provider grant | account/provider-link owner | broker exchange or existing native completion | account features | stored grant record | provider response is an observation; commit is fenced to the operation context |
+
+The composition root wires the broker, account owner, provider adapter, and
+existing native `MobileOAuth`. Provider SDK/storage details stay behind their
+respective adapters. No caller may use the handoff credential as an ordinary
+account session or native callback subject.
+
+## Contract and authorization context
+
+The broker exposes two narrow operations:
+
+1. `begin(account_id, provider, purpose, return_target)` validates that the
+   account is temporary and unexpired, creates one operation, and returns a
+   provider authorization URL plus an opaque handoff credential.
+2. `return(handoff, provider_result)` validates and atomically consumes the
+   handoff, rechecks the account and operation, exchanges the provider result,
+   and commits the grant only if the operation context is still valid.
+
+The handoff carries typed `operation_id`, temporary `account_id`, provider,
+purpose, issued-at, expiry, audience, and single-use/replay state. It is
+accepted only by the broker return endpoint for the same provider and purpose.
+It grants permission to complete that one linking operation; it cannot create
+an account session, access another account, change provider, or enter the
+permanent native callback path. The endpoint must reject malformed, expired,
+wrong-audience, wrong-purpose, already-consumed, and account-mismatch values
+without disclosing account or provider details.
+
+The broker revalidates account expiry, deletion, purpose, and operation state
+after the browser/provider wait and immediately before the grant commit. The
+commit uses an atomic operation/version precondition so expiry or revocation
+cannot race a successful write. If the provider exchange cannot be fenced
+atomically, the plan must document its revocation linearization point and the
+residual exposure; later local publication is suppressed after invalidation.
+
+## Expiry and privacy lifecycle
+
+Temporary accounts retain the product requirement of a 24-hour lifetime from
+creation. The implementation must store an absolute UTC expiry and use one
+clock/format at every boundary. Handoff expiry is shorter than the account
+lifetime and must be chosen from provider round-trip needs, with an explicit
+maximum and no silent extension. An operation cannot extend account lifetime.
+
+At account expiry, the lifecycle owner first revokes future broker writes and
+then removes or tombstones the account and its temporary operation/grant
+records according to the repository's retention decision. Delayed provider
+returns, retries, buffered telemetry, caches, and exports must fail the same
+epoch/tombstone fence and must not recreate state. Logs and diagnostics contain
+operation identifiers and outcome classes only; they inherit the account's
+sensitivity, purpose, retention, and erasure obligations. External provider
+copies are outside local erasure authority and must be reported as such.
+
+Required expiry cases are: begin near expiry; provider return after expiry;
+expiry during provider exchange; duplicate return; account deletion during a
+return; and a replacement account using the old operation identifier.
+
+## Native mobile compatibility boundary
+
+The existing native contract remains the default and sole path for permanent
+accounts: `MobileOAuth.begin` still requires a permanent registry subject, and
+`complete` still writes the existing provider grant shape. No shared callback
+change is required for released clients.
+
+The compatibility adapter is additive and temporary:
+
+- temporary-account callers select the browser broker explicitly;
+- permanent-account callers continue to select `native` and are rejected by
+  the broker unless an explicitly separate future contract is approved;
+- the adapter translates only broker completion into the account-link owner;
+- it never translates a handoff into a native callback subject or migrates
+  permanent grants;
+- provider names and callback versions are validated at the adapter boundary.
+
+The supported coexistence profile is old mobile clients using native OAuth,
+new temporary-account clients using the broker, and a backend capable of
+serving both. No rollback binary may interpret a broker handoff as a native
+callback. A feature/configuration switch may choose temporary-account routing,
+but it is an operational selector, not a second authorization mechanism.
+
+## Migration and rollout
+
+There is no bulk account migration. Existing permanent registry entries and
+provider grants remain untouched. The migration is an additive schema and
+contract rollout:
+
+1. Add the broker operation/credential representation and additive readers;
+   deploy storage and expiry fencing before issuing credentials.
+2. Deploy the backend adapter with issuance disabled, then enable it for a
+   bounded temporary-account cohort after synthetic contract checks pass.
+3. Observe return success, expiry rejection, replay rejection, provider
+   failures, and native-path invariants while old mobile clients continue on
+   native OAuth.
+4. Expand only after mixed-version evidence covers old native clients and the
+   new temporary browser path.
+
+Every operation transition is idempotent and compare-and-set fenced. An
+interrupted rollout leaves permanent records authoritative and temporary
+operations retryable or explicitly terminal; it must not leave dual grant
+authority. The compatibility adapter is removable after the temporary broker
+cohort is retired, all operations are terminal or expired, and telemetry shows
+no supported consumer for its contract. The broker itself has a separate
+removal decision and is not removed by native-flow rollback.
+
+## Rollback and cost
+
+Rollback disables new temporary broker issuance and routes new temporary
+linking attempts to a clear unsupported/maintenance response. It leaves
+already-issued handoffs valid only until their recorded expiry, unless an
+explicit revocation operation is available; the return endpoint remains for
+safe terminalization and replay rejection. In-flight provider exchanges are
+revalidated before any grant commit. Permanent native OAuth continues without
+rollback changes.
+
+The principal costs are:
+
+- one durable operation/credential representation, expiry cleanup, and
+  observability surface;
+- an additive broker endpoint and provider adapter contract;
+- mixed-version tests and support for old native clients during coexistence;
+- operational work to revoke or drain issued handoffs during rollback;
+- privacy review and retention/erasure evidence for temporary records,
+  logs, caches, and provider-disclosed data;
+- eventual cleanup of the adapter, storage, metrics, and runbook after the
+  removal gate is met.
+
+The main residual rollback risk is provider-side authorization already granted
+before local rollback; local rollback can prevent future local commits but
+cannot recall bytes or external provider state. The runbook must name the
+provider revocation action and its evidence separately from local rollback.
+
+## Quality scenarios and evidence
+
+1. **Temporary success** — a valid unexpired temporary account starts Polar
+   linking, completes once, and receives one grant; duplicate return produces
+   no second effect. Bound: one committed grant and one terminal operation.
+2. **Permanent compatibility** — an existing permanent account and old native
+   client complete the current callback path while the broker is enabled.
+   Bound: existing registry/grant behavior is unchanged.
+3. **Expiry fence** — expiry occurs between provider return and commit.
+   Expected: commit is rejected and no grant, cache, telemetry, or error body
+   discloses the account. Bound: no post-expiry local write.
+4. **Replay and confusion** — a handoff is replayed, presented to the wrong
+   provider/purpose/account, or sent to a native callback. Expected: reject
+   without escalation or cross-account effect.
+5. **Mixed rollout** — old native clients, new temporary browser clients, and
+   an older backend reader coexist during the supported window. Expected:
+   native requests remain native and broker records fail closed where the old
+   reader cannot interpret them.
+6. **Rollback** — issuance is disabled during an in-flight cohort. Expected:
+   permanent native linking continues; issued handoffs expire or are revoked;
+   no ambiguous operation state remains.
+
+Evidence must distinguish synthetic/provider-fake tests, storage migration
+checks, and any real provider/mobile release evidence. This plan does not claim
+real-provider or released-client proof; those classes remain pending until an
+authorized implementation and test campaign exists.
+
+## Assurance and implementation order
+
+Implementation owners are architecture foundations (ownership/composition),
+interfaces and events (broker and credential contract), and data and
+compatibility (expiry, records, coexistence, migration, and removal). Security
+assurance reviews the new trust boundary and handoff replay/audience/purpose
+threats. Privacy assurance reviews temporary identity and grant retention,
+expiry/erasure, logs, caches, and delayed writers. Independent assurance is
+required before any release claim; the current repository has only synthetic
+provider tests.
+
+The implementation sequence is: freeze the contract and schema; add focused
+owner tests for authorization and expiry fences; add the broker end-to-end
+slice; exercise mixed-version and rollback cases; run changed-path security
+and privacy reviews; then perform an authorized staged rollout. Production
+code is intentionally unchanged by this plan.
